{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A swelling, or a scab, or a bright spot</b> — these are names of lesions, and they are white, one differing from the other. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 13:2). And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 13:2) that \"שְׂאֵת\" is an expression of burning, from \"וְהַמַּשְׂאֵת הֵחֵלָּה\" (Judges 20:38) and \"וַיִּשָּׂאֵם דָּוִד\" (II Samuel 5:21), and it is possible that it is so called because the fire in its genesis tends to be raised upward. And \"סַפַּחַת\" is derived from \"סְפָחֵנִי נָא\" (I Samuel 2:36), \"וְנִסְפְּחוּ עַל בֵּית יַעֲקֹב\" (Isaiah 14:1) — an illness that gathers together into one place. \"בַּהֶרֶת\" is derived from \"בָּהִיר הוּא בַּשְּׁחָקִים\" (Job 37:21), that it takes on the form of a mark and a sign. If so, שְׂאֵת would be the name of the lesion produced from the greenish bile that burns, and בַּהֶרֶת from the white moisture, and סַפַּחַת that which is gathered from both of them. And our Rabbis said (Shevuot 6b): שְׂאֵת means nothing other than \"elevated,\" as it says, \"וְעַל כָּל הֶהָרִים הָרָמִים וְעַל כָּל הַגְּבָעוֹת הַנִּשָּׂאוֹת\" (Isaiah 2:14), and סַפַּחַת means nothing other than \"secondary/subsidiary,\" as it says, \"סְפָחֵנִי נָא\" (I Samuel 2:36)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:21.407543840Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}